\title{Finite Avoidance and Extremal Structure:\\Ramsey Counting, Conditional Survivors, and Balanced Tur\'an Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The probabilistic method proves existence by showing that forbidden configurations do not exhaust a finite candidate space. We develop this principle as exact finite combinatorics. Red--blue colorings of $K_n$ are represented by subsets of its edge set. Exact Boolean-lattice counts show that if $k\le n$ and
\[
2\binom nk<2^{\binom k2},
\]
then some coloring contains neither a red nor a blue $K_k$, so $R(k,k)>n$. The estimate $\binom nk\le n^k$ gives a simpler criterion and the concrete conclusion $R(10,10)>16$. We also prove a finite conditional-avoidance theorem: if every newly imposed bad set occupies strictly fewer than all outcomes surviving previous constraints, then a common survivor exists. This isolates the combinatorial core needed by local-lemma arguments without asserting an unproved dependency estimate or runtime claim. Finally, balanced complete bipartite graphs are triangle-free and have exactly $m^2$ edges on $2m$ vertices, attaining the Mantel bound. Algorithms for exhaustive Ramsey search, survivor filtering, and Tur\'an graph generation are analyzed. Random avoidance and deterministic extremality emerge as complementary cardinality principles.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Existence proofs in combinatorics frequently confront many interacting local constraints. A coloring must avoid every monochromatic clique; an assignment must avoid every bad event; a dense graph must avoid a forbidden subgraph. The probabilistic method changes the unit of analysis. Rather than design one candidate, it counts the whole candidate space.

For a finite set $\Omega$ and failure set $B\subseteq\Omega$, the inequality $|B|<|\Omega|$ implies that $\Omega\setminus B$ is nonempty. For a family $(B_i)$, the union estimate
\[
\left|\bigcup_iB_i\right|\le\sum_i|B_i|
\]
can establish the strict inequality. Probability is obtained by dividing these cardinalities by $|\Omega|$; the underlying existence argument remains finite.

This paper studies three manifestations of this idea. First, a coloring of $K_n$ is a point of a Boolean cube with $\binom n2$ coordinates. Requiring a fixed $k$-vertex set to be monochromatic fixes $\binom k2$ coordinates, giving an exact bad-event count. Second, conditional avoidance tracks candidates surviving an increasing list of constraints. Third, balanced complete bipartite graphs maximize the edge count compatible with triangle avoidance on an even number of vertices.

The exact contributions are a finite Ramsey criterion, the instance $R(10,10)>16$, a conditional-survivor theorem, and balanced two-part Tur\'an sharpness. The classical asymptotic scale near $2^{k/2}$, the symmetric Lov\'asz local lemma, efficient resampling bounds, and the general Tur\'an theorem are identified as extensions rather than claimed here.

\section{Definitions}
\begin{definition}
A simple graph $G=(V,E)$ has a finite vertex set $V$ and an edge set consisting of unordered pairs of distinct vertices. The complete graph $K_n$ contains all $\binom n2$ possible edges.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A red--blue coloring of $K_n$ assigns one of two colors to every edge. It is equivalently represented by the red-edge subset $R\subseteq E(K_n)$; its complement is blue.
\end{definition}

For a vertex set $T$ of size $k$, let $E(T)$ be its $\binom k2$ internal edges. Then $T$ spans a red $K_k$ precisely when $E(T)\subseteq R$, and it spans a blue $K_k$ precisely when $E(T)\cap R=\varnothing$.

\begin{definition}
The diagonal Ramsey number $R(k,k)$ is the least $N$ such that every red--blue coloring of $K_N$ contains a monochromatic $K_k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A graph is triangle-free if no three vertices are pairwise adjacent. The balanced complete bipartite graph $T_{2m,2}$ has two parts of size $m$, every edge between the parts, and no edge within a part.
\end{definition}

\section{Boolean-lattice counts}
\begin{lemma}[Superset interval count]
If $S\subseteq G$ are finite sets, the number of subsets $A\subseteq G$ satisfying $S\subseteq A$ is $2^{|G|-|S|}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every such $A$ is uniquely $S\cup B$ for a subset $B\subseteq G\setminus S$. There are $2^{|G|-|S|}$ choices for $B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Disjoint-subset count]
If $S\subseteq G$ are finite sets, the number of subsets $A\subseteq G$ satisfying $A\cap S=\varnothing$ is $2^{|G|-|S|}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Such sets are precisely the subsets of $G\setminus S$. Equivalently, complementation in $G$ bijects them with subsets containing $S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[A fixed monochromatic clique]
For a fixed $k$-vertex set $T$ in $K_n$, exactly
\[
2^{\binom n2-\binom k2}
\]
colorings make $T$ a red $K_k$, and the same number make it a blue $K_k$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Use the full edge set as $G$ and $E(T)$ as $S$. The red condition is containment and the blue condition is disjointness, so the preceding lemmas apply.
\end{proof}

\section{Ramsey avoidance by exact counting}
\begin{theorem}[Ramsey counting criterion]
Let $k\le n$. If
\[
2\binom nk<2^{\binom k2},
\]
then some red--blue coloring of $K_n$ contains no monochromatic $K_k$. Consequently $R(k,k)>n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
There are $2^{\binom n2}$ colorings. For each of the $\binom nk$ choices of $T$, the red and blue failure classes each have size $2^{\binom n2-\binom k2}$. Thus their union has cardinality at most
\[
2\binom nk2^{\binom n2-\binom k2}.
\]
The hypothesis makes this strictly smaller than
\[
2^{\binom k2}2^{\binom n2-\binom k2}=2^{\binom n2}.
\]
Hence the failure classes do not cover the coloring space, and a coloring outside their union has no monochromatic $K_k$.
\end{proof}

The assumption $k\le n$ supports the nonnegative exponent split. When $k>n$, the conclusion is true for the simpler reason that a $k$-clique cannot fit in $K_n$.

\begin{corollary}[Power criterion]
If $k\le n$ and
\[
2n^k<2^{\binom k2},
\]
then $R(k,k)>n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Use $\binom nk\le n^k$ and apply the Ramsey counting criterion.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Concrete diagonal bound]
There is a red--blue coloring of $K_{16}$ with no monochromatic $K_{10}$. Hence $R(10,10)>16$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
We have $10\le16$ and
\[
2\cdot16^{10}=2^{41}<2^{45}=2^{\binom{10}{2}}.
\]
The power criterion applies.
\end{proof}

If $X$ counts monochromatic $k$-vertex sets in a uniformly random coloring, then
\[
\mathbb E[X]=\binom nk2^{1-\binom k2}.
\]
The counting criterion is exactly $\mathbb E[X]<1$. Since $X$ is a nonnegative integer, some coloring has $X=0$. This normalized formulation and the cardinality proof are the same argument.

The criterion does not by itself supply the full uniform asymptotic lower bound near $2^{k/2}$. That scale requires sharper estimates for $\binom nk$; the exact event count isolates the remaining arithmetic task.

\section{Conditional survival}
Let $\Omega$ be a nonempty finite outcome set, $I$ a finite constraint set, and $B_i\subseteq\Omega$ the bad outcomes for constraint $i$. For $S\subseteq I$, define
\[
\operatorname{Surv}(S)=\{\omega\in\Omega:\omega\notin B_i\text{ for all }i\in S\}.
\]

\begin{lemma}[One-step filtering]
For $S\subseteq I$ and $i\in I$,
\[
\operatorname{Surv}(S\cup\{i\})=\operatorname{Surv}(S)\setminus B_i.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Both sides consist exactly of outcomes avoiding every $B_j$ with $j\in S$ and also avoiding $B_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Strict loss leaves a survivor]
If
\[
|\operatorname{Surv}(S)\cap B_i|<|\operatorname{Surv}(S)|,
\]
then $\operatorname{Surv}(S\cup\{i\})$ is nonempty.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise every current survivor would lie in $B_i$, forcing equality of the two displayed cardinalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite conditional-avoidance principle]
Assume that for every $S\subseteq I$ and $i\in I\setminus S$, whenever $\operatorname{Surv}(S)$ is nonempty,
\[
|\operatorname{Surv}(S)\cap B_i|<|\operatorname{Surv}(S)|.
\]
Then there exists $\omega\in\Omega$ avoiding every $B_i$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Order $I$ as $i_1,\ldots,i_r$. Initially $\operatorname{Surv}(\varnothing)=\Omega$ is nonempty. Inductively, strict loss and the preceding lemma show that adding $i_{j+1}$ preserves nonemptiness. Thus $\operatorname{Surv}(I)$ is nonempty.
\end{proof}

This theorem isolates the finite engine of conditional avoidance. It is not the symmetric Lov\'asz local lemma. In particular, it does not derive its hypothesis from event probabilities bounded by $p$, dependency degree bounded by $d$, and $ep(d+1)\le1$. Such a derivation would require an additional lower bound on conditional survivor ratios. Nor does the theorem establish a resampling runtime; efficient variable-model search requires further witness-tree analysis.

\section{Balanced Tur\'an sharpness}
\begin{theorem}[Balanced Tur\'an sharpness]
For every natural number $m$, the graph $T_{2m,2}$ is triangle-free and has exactly $m^2$ edges. Equivalently,
\[
4|E(T_{2m,2})|=(2m)^2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Among any three vertices, two lie in the same part and are nonadjacent, so no triangle exists. Every edge is uniquely determined by one endpoint in each part, yielding $m\cdot m=m^2$ edges.
\end{proof}

Mantel's theorem states that every triangle-free graph on $N$ vertices has at most $\lfloor N^2/4\rfloor$ edges. For $N=2m$, the theorem above gives the equality construction. The general Tur\'an theorem maximizes edges in a $K_{r+1}$-free graph using a balanced complete $r$-partite graph, with leading count
\[
\left(1-\frac1r\right)\frac{n^2}{2}.
\]
Our exact assertion is the balanced case $r=2$ with even $n$.

\begin{theorem}[Ramsey--Tur\'an finite synthesis]
There exists a red--blue coloring of $K_{16}$ with no monochromatic $K_{10}$, and for every $m$, the graph $T_{2m,2}$ is triangle-free with exactly $m^2$ edges.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine the concrete Ramsey bound with balanced Tur\'an sharpness.
\end{proof}

The conjunction compares complementary modes of extremal combinatorics. Ramsey avoidance proves that forbidden coloring cylinders fail to cover a Boolean cube. Tur\'an sharpness organizes edge support so that a forbidden triangle is impossible while density meets its exact ceiling.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Exhaustive Ramsey search}
Number the $M=\binom n2$ edges. Each $M$-bit integer represents a coloring. For each coloring and each $k$-vertex subset, inspect its $\binom k2$ internal edges. Accept the first coloring for which these edges are neither all red nor all blue. Under the counting criterion, the search must terminate successfully. Its worst-case running time is
\[
O\!\left(2^{\binom n2}\binom nk\binom k2\right),
\]
and it uses $O(\binom n2)$ working space apart from enumeration overhead. It is a finite witness-extraction procedure, not an efficient large-scale algorithm.

\subsection{Conditional survivor filtering}
Represent $\Omega$ explicitly. For each constraint $i$, delete every current outcome in $B_i$. The filtering lemma shows that the stored set always equals the outcomes satisfying all processed constraints. The conditional-avoidance hypothesis prevents emptiness. With constant-time membership tests, the cost is at most $O(|\Omega||I|)$ time and $O(|\Omega|)$ space.

\subsection{Balanced Tur\'an generation}
Create vertices $0,\ldots,2m-1$, place the first $m$ in one part, and output every pair with one endpoint in each part. The algorithm emits $m^2$ edges in $\Theta(m^2)$ time and output space, optimal up to constants for an explicit edge list.

\section{Applications and interpretation}
The Ramsey argument applies whenever a forbidden pattern fixes a known number of independent binary coordinates. Analogous cylinder counts occur in coding theory, randomized constructions, constraint satisfaction, and property testing. The conditional theorem does not need product structure and can model schedules, assignments, or configurations subject to finitely many tests, though its conditional hypothesis may be demanding.

Balanced bipartite graphs model maximally dense two-class interaction networks without three-way mutual adjacency. Every cross-class connection is retained, while the absence of within-class edges precludes triangles automatically.

Three notions of constructivity should be distinguished. An exact finite count first places a witness in an explicitly enumerable universe. Exhaustive enumeration then gives a terminating search. Efficient construction requires a favorable complexity bound. Ramsey counting establishes the first two levels but not efficient search. Balanced Tur\'an graphs have a direct output-sensitive construction. Conditional filtering is efficient only when the outcome space itself is explicitly manageable.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The red and blue clique events are dual Boolean-lattice classes: an upper interval and a disjointness class related by complementation. Conditional survival replaces one global estimate with a sequence of local estimates; merely knowing that each bad set is proper is insufficient, because it might contain every outcome left by earlier constraints.

Several extensions are natural. First, one may seek to prove that probability at most $p$, dependency degree at most $d$, and $ep(d+1)\le1$ imply a positive conditional survivor ratio, thereby obtaining the symmetric local lemma through finite induction. Second, witness-tree generating functions may bound the expected number of resamplings in a variable model. Third, sharper binomial estimates may promote the exact Ramsey count to the classical exponential scale. Fourth, an entropy functional may connect the volume of forbidden coloring cylinders with balanced Tur\'an maximizers. Finally, stability theory should quantify how close a nearly extremal triangle-free graph must be to bipartite.

\section{Conclusion}
A fixed monochromatic $K_k$ occupies exactly $2^{\binom n2-\binom k2}$ colorings in either color. Summing these failure classes gives the criterion $2\binom nk<2^{\binom k2}$ and the concrete consequence $R(10,10)>16$. In an arbitrary finite outcome space, strict conditional loss guarantees a common survivor by induction. At the deterministic extremal pole, balanced complete bipartite graphs avoid triangles while attaining $m^2$ edges on $2m$ vertices. The shared lesson is cardinal: describe the candidate universe, calculate the reach of prohibition, and prove either that room remains or that an extremal construction meets the boundary exactly.

\end{document}

